NAME
    bootsettings - view and edit the boot configuration (/etc/boot.cfg)

SYNOPSIS
    bootsettings
    bootsettings show
    bootsettings profile   <minimal|normal|full|diagnostic>
    bootsettings verbosity <silent|splash|text|verbose|auto>
    bootsettings show      <on|off>
    bootsettings cputier   <auto|1|2|3>
    bootsettings <feature> <auto|on|off>
    bootsettings reset

DESCRIPTION
    bootsettings edits the boot spectrum that controls how TOS starts. Changes
    are written to /etc/boot.cfg and apply on the next boot. The same settings
    can be edited visually by pressing DELETE during the boot POST screen.

    Two independent dials:
      profile    WHAT LOADS. minimal = bare shell; normal = optional subsystems
                 load if RAM allows (the default); full = load everything;
                 diagnostic = full + integrity check + verbose log.
      verbosity  WHAT TOS SAYS while booting (a "muter", not a behavior change):
                 silent, splash, text (default), verbose. Use 'auto' to follow
                 the profile's default.

    advanced toggles (one per optional feature: swap, power, net, theme, compat,
    audio, integrityCheck) override the profile: 'on' forces load, 'off' forces
    skip, 'auto' follows the profile. cputier confirms/corrects the detected CPU
    tier; showConfig toggles the boot POST screen.

    With no argument (or 'show'), bootsettings prints the current settings and
    the detected hardware. Requires an ADMIN session to change anything.

EXAMPLES
    bootsettings                 Show current settings + hardware.
    bootsettings profile full    Load every available subsystem next boot.
    bootsettings verbosity verbose
    bootsettings cputier 2       Pin the CPU tier (correct a wrong estimate).
    bootsettings net off         Skip the network stack regardless of profile.

SEE ALSO
    man boot, MANUAL.md (Chapter 2)
